 ],
 "literal": "Foolish indeed are the curved-stature cassia trees! / Before the season promised by him who went through the stone-abounding desert path has arrived, / to break forth, / they blossomed banner-like clusters joining the branches, / mistaking the unseasonal rain for the rainy season!",
 "translation": "Foolish indeed are the broad-trunked cassia trees! Mistaking the unseasonal rain for the actual rainy season, they have burst into bloom, their banner-like clusters crowding the branches, even before the time promised by him who went through the stony desert has arrived.",
 "modern": "கற்கள் நிறைந்த பாலைவனப் பாதையில் சென்ற காதலர் குறிப்பிட்ட கார்காலம் இன்னும் வராத நிலையிலும், பெய்த புதிய தற்காலிக மழையைக் கார்காலம் என்று தவறாக நினைத்து, கிளைகளில் கொத்துக் கொத்தாகப் பூக்களைப் பூத்துவிட்ட இந்தக் கொன்றை மரங்கள் நிச்சயமாக அறிவில்லாதவை ஆகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the mullai landscape, which is associated with the rainy season and the patient waiting of the heroine. The speaker is the heroine's close friend, who is consoling her. The hero had promised to return by the onset of the rainy season, but a sudden, unseasonal shower falls, causing the cassia trees to burst into golden blossoms. Seeing this, the heroine is distressed, thinking the promised season has arrived and her lover has failed to return. The friend cleverly reassures her by calling the cassia trees foolish for mistaking a passing shower for the actual rainy season. By blaming the trees rather than the lover, she implies that the true season of return has not yet begun and that the hero will keep his word.",
